p was dug out by (the king) Sagara.* And the great ocean shall surely help Rāma belonging to Sagara's race. *Sagara was a sovereign of Ayodhyā, belonging to the same family with Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.724,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-19-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6013014","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b951ac195fdd9ca7495931a3796eef6b2702e81c3c3f11cfa782e46f5f3cf44d","compact":"907c0f194be58e7d88dd18584c27ce87a17046fead1bd5abd6ffe97ee012535e","nasal":"907c0f194be58e7d88dd18584c27ce87a17046fead1bd5abd6ffe97ee012535e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-13-15","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":13,"verse":15,"english":"Being thus accosted by the wise Rākşasa, Vibhīşaņa. Sugrīva reached where Rāma with Laksmana was. And those words pleased Răma, pious by nature. And the highly effulgent (hero) requested, smiling, the active Lakşmaņa and the monkey-chief Sugrīva to collect necessaries for his adoration.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.3,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-19-27","DUTT-6-19-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6013015","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"21456c504caeb161ead0e5fc71df0ab217a970a0a664fce55c8af2d71c59044f","compact":"b80427900a3dee1bcad751fe5768891bbc9805a42266c2c511fe79409b872617","nasal":"b80427900a3dee1bcad751fe5768891bbc9805a42266c2c511fe79409b872617"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-13-16","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":13,"verse":16,"english":"And those words pleased Răma, pious by nature. And the highly effulgent (hero) requested, smiling, the active Lakşmaņa and the monkey-chief Sugrīva to collect necessaries for his adoration.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.662,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-19-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6013016","sanskrit